-- title:
--   Unit discriminant of the Vélu quotient by a p-torsion line
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring, let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $A$ with coefficients $a_1,\dots,a_6$, and let $p$ be a prime with $p \neq 2$. Assume that $p \cdot \Delta(W)$ is a unit of $A$, and let $x \in A$ be a root of the $p$-th division polynomial, i.e. $(W.\mathrm{pre}\Psi\,p)(x) = 0$ in Mathlib's normalisation. The conclusion is that the discriminant of the Weierstrass curve [`ModularCurve.LevelP.quotientByLine W p x`](def/ModularCurve_KatzLevelPQuotient.html#L45) is a unit of $A$. Here that curve is defined purely in terms of $x$: writing $x_a = \Phi_a(x) \cdot \mathrm{inverse}(\Psi_a^2(x))$ for $1 \le a \le (p-1)/2$, where $\Phi_a, \Psi_a^2$ are the $x$-coordinate numerator and denominator polynomials of multiplication by $a$ and $\mathrm{inverse}$ is the total inverse function on $A$, and setting $T = \sum_{a=1}^{(p-1)/2} \bigl(6x_a^2 + b_2 x_a + b_4\bigr)$ and $W' = \sum_{a=1}^{(p-1)/2}\bigl(\Psi_2^2(x_a) + x_a(6x_a^2 + b_2x_a + b_4)\bigr)$, the quotient curve has the same $a_1, a_2, a_3$ as $W$, while $a_4$ is replaced by $a_4 - 5T$ and $a_6$ by $a_6 - b_2 T - 7W'$. Thus the assertion is that these Vélu-type formulae, evaluated at a root of the $p$-division polynomial over an arbitrary base in which $p\Delta(W)$ is invertible, again produce a curve with invertible discriminant.
--
--   The formulae defining the quotient curve are Vélu's isogeny formulae for the quotient by the subgroup generated by a point of order $p$, rewritten so that only the $x$-coordinate of that point occurs and so that they make sense over an arbitrary commutative ring. In contrast with the textbook statement, which concerns an elliptic curve over a field and a point of exact order $p$, the hypotheses here are the purely algebraic ones that $p\,\Delta(W)$ be invertible and that $x$ be a root of the $p$-division polynomial; no point of the curve, and no geometric assumption on $\operatorname{Spec} A$, is assumed. The resulting invertibility of $\Delta$ is what allows the quotient curve to be fed into the functor defining Katz modular forms, and it is used in the construction of a Katz form of weight $2$ on $\Gamma_0(p)$ with prescribed $q$-expansion at the cusp from a classical modular form on $\Gamma_0(p)$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_LevelP_isUnit_discriminant_quotientByLine.lean

